# AI models — all seven are REQUIRED. .envrc refuses to load without them.
# .opencode/opencode.json pins every agent to one of these abstract names, so a
# missing value stops OpenCode rather than silently degrading. Model IDs are not
# secrets; only the provider credential below is.
#
# MODEL_A  orchestration, expert advisors, and the global default
# MODEL_B  requirements, the two tester gates, expert advisors
# MODEL_C  authoring — DB, DevOps, Performance Engineer. No DoD gate may sit here.
# MODEL_D  small_model only (titles, summaries); no agent uses it
# MODEL_E  UI Builder
# MODEL_F  two review gates that audit MODEL_A's own output — Architecture
#          Guardian and Security Auditor
# MODEL_G  the code-review gate — @code-reviewer alone. A sixth distinct model,
#          so that no gate shares a model identifier with an agent authoring the
#          code it reviews (see below)
#
# MODEL_C and MODEL_E must be a DIFFERENT model family from MODEL_A and MODEL_B,
# so that no delegated production artefact is reviewed by the family that wrote
# it. Note MODEL_E is currently the SAME model ID as MODEL_C, so "MODEL_C/MODEL_E"
# names one model and MODEL_E is equally ineligible for a DoD gate. MODEL_F must
# differ in family from MODEL_A — because MODEL_A authors Java directly and
# MODEL_F reviews it — and also from MODEL_C/MODEL_E, which author migrations, CI
# and the front end. MODEL_G must differ in family from every tier that authors
# code — MODEL_A and MODEL_B, and MODEL_C/MODEL_E — because @code-reviewer
# reviews production, infrastructure and test code alike. Since 2026-08-24
# (EOP-179) the invariant holds with ZERO exceptions FOR PRODUCTION CODE,
# INFRASTRUCTURE AND TEST CODE: @code-reviewer moved off MODEL_B onto MODEL_G, so
# it no longer shares a model identifier with the MODEL_B tester gates whose tests
# it reviews. It is NOT unconditional. @security-auditor and @architecture-guardian
# still share MODEL_F, and @architecture-guardian AUTHORS ADRs and C4 models, so
# ARCHITECTURE DOCUMENTATION is still reviewed by a tier-mate at one model
# identifier — neither model- nor family-independent. That overlap is a declared,
# justified allow-list entry in SeparationInvariantTest. See Blueprint §3.1/§3.4,
# ADR-022, ADR-046 and ADR-059.
#
# A DoD gate may only be pinned to a model that has PASSED the two-stage probe in
# ADR-046 — invocability in-region, then an observed tool call whose answer is
# checked against independently established ground truth. Catalogue metadata is
# not sufficient evidence: three models satisfying every documented capability
# clause on paper failed that probe. Verdicts go in Blueprint §3.4.1, and a gate
# pin with no recorded passing verdict is non-compliant.
#
# To switch provider, run: ./tools/switch-provider.sh [zen|bedrock|go|go-free],
# then restart opencode. `go --free-only` is the same as `go-free`. For the Zen,
# Go and Bedrock blocks that script only moves the leading `#`, so keep the
# `# === Zen ===`, `# === Go ===`, `# === Go Free ===`, `# === Bedrock ===` and
# `# AWS` marker lines intact, and keep every model line starting at column 0
# with either MODEL_ or #MODEL_. The `# === Go Free ===` block is the exception:
# `go-free` fetches the live catalogue, filters zero-cost models and rewrites
# that block, so its IDs below are a snapshot for reference and may lag. Flags:
# --offline (use the existing block, no fetch), --refresh (rewrite the Go Free
# block only, no toggle), --dry-run (print what would change, touch nothing).
# Run `./tools/switch-provider.sh agents` to print the model assigned to each
# agent (delivery + expert) under the currently active block, without changing
# anything.

# === Zen ===
# OpenCode Zen needs no key in .env: authenticate with `/connect` in the TUI and
# the key is stored in ~/.local/share/opencode/auth.json. See Blueprint §3.4.
#MODEL_A=opencode/claude-opus-5
#MODEL_B=opencode/claude-sonnet-4-6
#MODEL_C=opencode/gpt-5.3-codex
#MODEL_D=opencode/gemini-3.5-flash-lite
#MODEL_E=opencode/gemini-3.1-pro
# Same model as the Bedrock MODEL_F below, but reached through a different
# provider, so it is NOT probed on this route. ADR-046 requires a passing probe
# before a gate runs on it.
#MODEL_F=opencode/minimax-m2.5
# Same model as the Bedrock MODEL_G below, but reached through a different
# provider, so it is NOT probed on this route either. ADR-046 requires a passing
# probe before a gate runs on it.
#MODEL_G=opencode/glm-5

# === Go ===
# OpenCode Go is the $10/mo subscription: authenticate with `/connect` in the
# TUI (OpenCode Go + API key). Model IDs use the `opencode-go/<id>` prefix.
# Live catalogue: https://opencode.ai/zen/go/v1/models — re-check it before
# relying on any ID here. None of these is probed on this route, so ADR-046
# bars a DoD gate from running on them until a passing probe is recorded.
#MODEL_A=opencode-go/kimi-k3
#MODEL_B=opencode-go/gpt-6-luna
#MODEL_C=opencode-go/deepseek-v4.1-flash
#MODEL_D=opencode-go/glm-5.3-flash
#MODEL_E=opencode-go/deepseek-v4.1-flash
#MODEL_F=opencode-go/glm-5.3
#MODEL_G=opencode-go/muse-spark-1.3-contributor

# === Go Free ===
# Same provider, free models only. The block below is regenerated from the live
# catalogue by `./tools/switch-provider.sh go-free`; the IDs are a snapshot and
# may lag. Only three IDs cost $0/$0 at the time of writing, so tiers repeat and
# family separation between authors and review gates is NOT met — use for cheap
# exploration, not as a gate-compliant configuration. Some free models also
# require Global regions in workspace privacy settings and/or training-data
# consent; re-check https://opencode.ai/zen/go/v1/models before relying on any ID.
#MODEL_A=opencode-go/beam-free
#MODEL_B=opencode-go/iceman-contributor-free
#MODEL_C=opencode-go/longcat-2.5-free
#MODEL_D=opencode-go/beam-free
#MODEL_E=opencode-go/iceman-contributor-free
#MODEL_F=opencode-go/longcat-2.5-free
#MODEL_G=opencode-go/beam-free

# === Bedrock ===
# Copy IDs verbatim from ~/.cache/opencode/models.json. Anthropic IDs carry no
# -v1:0 suffix while most third-party ones do, and a wrong suffix looks exactly
# like a missing entitlement. Verify any new model with:
#   opencode run --model amazon-bedrock/<id> "Reply with exactly: OK"
# then a tool-using prompt — several non-Anthropic models pass plain text but
# emit malformed tool calls. Verdicts are recorded in Blueprint §3.4.1.
MODEL_A=amazon-bedrock/global.anthropic.claude-opus-5
MODEL_B=amazon-bedrock/eu.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6
MODEL_C=amazon-bedrock/qwen.qwen3-coder-480b-a35b-v1:0
MODEL_D=amazon-bedrock/eu.anthropic.claude-haiku-4-5-20251001-v1:0
MODEL_E=amazon-bedrock/qwen.qwen3-coder-480b-a35b-v1:0
MODEL_F=amazon-bedrock/minimax.minimax-m2.5
MODEL_G=amazon-bedrock/zai.glm-5

# AWS — Bedrock only. A bearer token is what this project actually uses; the
# IAM key pair is the SigV4 alternative and is left empty. Note that with a
# bearer token the AWS CLI control plane is unavailable, so
# `aws bedrock list-foundation-models` cannot be used to check entitlements.
AWS_BEARER_TOKEN_BEDROCK=your-bedrock-bearer-token
AWS_REGION=eu-west-2
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# Required
JAVA_HOME=/usr/local/opt/openjdk@21/libexec/openjdk.jdk/Contents/Home
GITHUB_TOKEN=ghp_example0123456789abcdef0123456789abcde
JIRA_URL=https://your-domain.atlassian.net
JIRA_PROJECT_KEY=EOP
JIRA_USERNAME=you@example.com
JIRA_API_TOKEN=ATATT3xFfGF0exampleTokenValue123=

# Optional
SUPERMEMORY_API_KEY=sm_exampleKey0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef0123

# Grafana (admin account seeded on first boot; pick strong passwords)
# Wrap passwords in single quotes if they contain $ (compose/direnv interpolation)
GF_SECURITY_ADMIN_USER=admin
GF_SECURITY_ADMIN_PASSWORD=CHANGE_ME

# Database (local dev defaults shown; production uses DATASOURCE_* in application-prod.yml)
DB_URL=jdbc:h2:mem:eop
DB_USERNAME=sa
DB_PASSWORD=

# Local Swagger UI opt-in (EOP-38, ADR-049). Both surfaces ship disabled in
# application.yml so that omitting configuration fails closed; these two variables are the
# sanctioned way to turn them back on for local work. .envrc runs `dotenv`, so copying this
# file is enough — `./mvnw spring-boot:run` then serves /v3/api-docs and /swagger-ui.html.
# Delete or set them to false to see exactly what the container serves.
#
# compose.app.yml has no `env_file:`, so these are never injected into the app container;
# the deployed stack stays hardened whatever your .env says. Do not add them to
# compose.app.yml — application-prod.yml pins both to false as a second guard anyway.
SPRINGDOC_APIDOCS_ENABLED=true
SPRINGDOC_SWAGGERUI_ENABLED=true

# InfluxDB — where `test/k6/run.sh` streams load-test metrics.
# This is a HOST address, and both halves matter: k6 runs on your machine rather than
# inside the Compose network, so the hostname must be `localhost`, and the k6 InfluxDB
# output takes the database from the URL path, so `/k6` must be present.
# Grafana does NOT read this variable. It reaches InfluxDB at the fixed container
# address pinned in tools/monitoring/grafana/datasources/datasource.yml, because
# `localhost` inside the Grafana container is Grafana itself (ADR-016).
# There are no InfluxDB credentials: the stack binds to 127.0.0.1 and runs with HTTP
# authentication disabled, so nothing here needs a username or a password.
# After editing this file run `direnv reload` — direnv exported the old value into
# every shell that is already open, so k6 would keep writing to the previous address.
INFLUXDB_URL=http://localhost:8086/k6

# Containerised stack (compose.app.yml). Used by `docker compose -f compose.app.yml up`.
# On EC2 these same variables are written to /opt/eop/.env by Terraform user-data.
POSTGRES_DB=eop
POSTGRES_USER=eop
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=CHANGE_ME
# Pin overridden by infra/variables.tf on the instance; this is the local default.
POSTGRES_IMAGE=postgres:17-alpine
# Named volume locally; an absolute EBS-backed path on the instance.
# POSTGRES_DATA_DIR=eop_postgres_data
# Build locally with `docker build -t eop-threat-modeling:local .`, or point at an
# immutable GHCR tag published by CI.
APP_IMAGE=eop-threat-modeling:local
# Front end + Caddy reverse proxy. Build locally with `docker build -t eop-ui:local ui`.
UI_IMAGE=eop-ui:local
# 127.0.0.1 locally so nothing is exposed off the machine; 0.0.0.0 on the instance.
# This binds Caddy on port 443 (EOP-21; was port 80) - the app container publishes
# no host port at all (see docs/adr/ADR-017-frontend-delivery-topology.md).
APP_BIND=127.0.0.1

# Production datasource (application-prod.yml). compose.app.yml OVERRIDES these with
# the PostgreSQL service name, because a localhost URL inside the app container means
# the container itself. They are listed here for a bare `java -jar -Dspring.profiles.active=prod`.
DATASOURCE_URL=jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/eop
DATASOURCE_USER=eop
DATASOURCE_PASSWORD=CHANGE_ME
